        "question": {
          ":question/id": "324afc1f-b2da-50ed-b0b3-f131d231a610",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}=6 y \\sin{(x )} + 5\\,.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}=6 y \\sin{(x )} + 5 \\\\ 3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 6 y \\sin{(x )}=4 \\cos{(x )} + 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- 2 \\sin{(x )})}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{\\frac{4 \\cos{(x )}}{3} + \\frac{5}{3}}_{Q(x)}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- 2 \\sin{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{4 \\cos{(x )}}{3} + \\frac{5}{3}.$",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
